1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,658
Your team squash-merges every feature
branch. Last week you squashed Asha's

2
00:00:09,698 --> 00:00:15,411
rationale branch into main. This week she
added one more commit to the same branch,

3
00:00:15,451 --> 00:00:20,817
and you squash it again. Git stops with
a conflict, on a line that last week's

4
00:00:20,857 --> 00:00:23,381
squash had already delivered to main.

5
00:00:23,721 --> 00:00:28,113
Nobody edited that line on main. Git is
in conflict with its own earlier merge.

6
00:00:28,153 --> 00:00:32,714
The cause is one missing parent link, and
when you can see that link, you can also

7
00:00:32,754 --> 00:00:37,426
see what every merge option is really
choosing. Hold on to that missing parent link.

8
00:00:39,266 --> 00:00:43,382
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

9
00:00:43,422 --> 00:00:48,628
A merge joins another branch's history
into yours. In the last two videos the

10
00:00:48,668 --> 00:00:54,215
question was how to resolve one. Today the
question is what kind of result you ask

11
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:59,597
for. Four options decide whether a merge
creates a commit, what kind, and when:

12
00:00:59,637 --> 00:01:05,252
--ff-only, --no-ff, --no-commit and --squash.
They don't change the merged content.

13
00:01:05,292 --> 00:01:07,977
They change the history that records it.

14
00:01:08,317 --> 00:01:13,172
Then you open a merge commit and read it
as an object: one tree, more than one

15
00:01:13,212 --> 00:01:16,499
parent, and an order of
parents that carries meaning.

16
00:01:16,539 --> 00:01:20,014
A parent is the commit that
another commit was built on.

17
00:01:20,054 --> 00:01:24,972
That order is what git log --first-parent
reads. And you close with the octopus

18
00:01:25,012 --> 00:01:27,671
merge, a commit with
three or more parents.

19
00:01:28,011 --> 00:01:34,883
All of this is Git. One callout near the
end crosses to GitHub, and the label on

20
00:01:34,923 --> 00:01:36,525
screen will say so.

21
00:01:38,365 --> 00:01:39,944
Here's what you'll walk away with.

22
00:01:40,284 --> 00:01:41,696
After this video you can:

23
00:01:42,036 --> 00:01:49,621
Choose between --ff-only, --no-ff,
--no-commit and --squash for a stated goal.

24
00:01:49,961 --> 00:01:56,101
Explain what a squash merge leaves behind
and why the branch cannot be reused

25
00:01:56,141 --> 00:01:56,663
safely.

26
00:01:57,003 --> 00:02:00,151
Read a merge commit: two parents,
and a diff against each.

27
00:02:00,491 --> 00:02:04,829
Explain what --first-parent shows and
why the order of parents matters.

28
00:02:05,169 --> 00:02:07,243
Say when an octopus merge is possible.

29
00:02:09,083 --> 00:02:12,706
A merge produces two separate things: a
tree, which is the merged content, and

30
00:02:12,746 --> 00:02:16,134
ancestry, which is the set of parent
links on the commit that records it.

31
00:02:16,174 --> 00:02:19,750
The options of this video leave the tree
alone and decide about the ancestry.

32
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:24,889
Row by row. A fast-forward, remember,
moves your branch name forward and creates

33
00:02:24,929 --> 00:02:29,606
nothing new. With no option, Git
fast-forwards when it can and creates a merge

34
00:02:29,646 --> 00:02:34,566
commit when it can't. --no-ff forces a
merge commit where a fast-forward would do.

35
00:02:34,606 --> 00:02:38,800
The branch stays visible in the graph
and can be reverted as one unit.

36
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:44,329
--ff-only either fast-forwards or moves
nothing. --no-commit is meant to let you

37
00:02:44,369 --> 00:02:49,427
inspect a merge before it's recorded,
and it has a gap you'll see in the demo.

38
00:02:49,467 --> 00:02:54,787
--squash computes the same result as a
merge and then deliberately forgets that it

39
00:02:54,827 --> 00:02:55,310
was one.

40
00:02:55,650 --> 00:03:03,209
merge.ff sets the default: false behaves
like --no-ff, only like --ff-only.

41
00:03:03,549 --> 00:03:08,231
Now the merge commit itself. In one sentence:
a merge commit is an ordinary commit

42
00:03:08,271 --> 00:03:12,896
object with more than one parent line, and
the order of those lines records which

43
00:03:12,936 --> 00:03:14,393
branch received the merge.

44
00:03:14,733 --> 00:03:18,988
Precisely: a merge commit stores a
complete tree, like every commit.

45
00:03:19,028 --> 00:03:23,282
It stores no diff, no conflict record
and no list of merged commits.

46
00:03:23,322 --> 00:03:27,450
Parent 1 is the commit HEAD pointed
to when the merge was created.

47
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:29,913
Parent 2 is the commit that was merged.

48
00:03:30,253 --> 00:03:36,174
On screen, the merge is 821b8ff, its
parent 1 is c089834 and its parent 2 is

49
00:03:36,214 --> 00:03:42,213
58a5e60. M^1 and M^2 name them. M^1..M^2
is the set of commits that the merge

50
00:03:42,253 --> 00:03:47,390
brought in, and M^- is shorthand
for the same range plus M itself.

51
00:03:47,730 --> 00:03:50,706
Inside .git that is one commit object. No
flag and no separate storage distinguishes

52
00:03:50,746 --> 00:03:51,783
a merge from any other commit.

53
00:03:52,123 --> 00:03:55,734
git log --first-parent follows
only parent 1 at every merge.

54
00:03:55,774 --> 00:04:00,054
On a main branch that receives work
through merges, that is the list of

55
00:04:00,094 --> 00:04:04,071
integrations, one line each. Here
it skips the two branch commits.

56
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:08,033
And the octopus: naming several branches
in one git merge creates a single commit

57
00:04:08,073 --> 00:04:11,740
with three or more parents, made by a
separate strategy that refuses any conflict.

58
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:15,913
When not to use which? --squash
on a branch that lives on.

59
00:04:15,953 --> 00:04:21,055
--no-commit alone when you need a
guaranteed stop. An octopus when any of the

60
00:04:21,095 --> 00:04:24,594
branches might conflict. The
demo shows each failure.

61
00:04:26,434 --> 00:04:28,588
First, one sentence to carry with you.

62
00:04:28,928 --> 00:04:32,850
The textbook's analogy for a merge
commit is a river and a tributary.

63
00:04:32,890 --> 00:04:37,329
Downstream of the confluence there is
one river, and the map still shows which

64
00:04:37,369 --> 00:04:41,808
channel was the main stream. First-parent
history is following the main stream

65
00:04:41,848 --> 00:04:46,631
upstream and ignoring every tributary. On
screen, M is the confluence and feature is

66
00:04:46,671 --> 00:04:47,435
the tributary.

67
00:04:47,775 --> 00:04:51,256
Quick quiz. Which commit
becomes parent 1 of a merge?

68
00:04:51,296 --> 00:04:56,039
A, the tip of main. B, whatever was
checked out when the merge was made.

69
00:04:56,079 --> 00:04:56,836
Your answer?

70
00:05:01,676 --> 00:05:07,405
It's B, and that's where the river
breaks. In Git the "main stream" is not a

71
00:05:07,445 --> 00:05:13,706
property of a branch name. It's whichever
commit was checked out when the merge was

72
00:05:13,746 --> 00:05:20,006
made. Merge main into a feature branch and
the feature branch is the main stream of

73
00:05:20,046 --> 00:05:23,953
that merge commit, whatever
the branches are called.

74
00:05:23,993 --> 00:05:26,155
You will watch that go wrong.

75
00:05:26,495 --> 00:05:31,274
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
A, a branch with B and C, and a squash

76
00:05:31,314 --> 00:05:34,844
commit S on main. How many lines
go into S? Say it out loud.

77
00:05:39,684 --> 00:05:49,136
One line, from A. For the squash, extend
the map. A squash merge is water arriving

78
00:05:49,176 --> 00:05:58,281
in the river with no channel drawn on the
map. The river is fuller, and the map

79
00:05:58,321 --> 00:06:07,773
doesn't say from where. The merge base,
the best common ancestor, is computed from

80
00:06:07,813 --> 00:06:11,014
the map, not from the water.

81
00:06:12,854 --> 00:06:20,641
M and S record the same tree. Only
M records where it came from.

82
00:06:20,981 --> 00:06:23,048
And the merge commit from a moment
ago, with both parents spelled out.

83
00:06:24,888 --> 00:06:28,731
--no-ff. main is an ancestor of the branch,
so a plain merge would fast-forward.

84
00:06:28,771 --> 00:06:32,565
git merge 🟡 CAUTION: it moves the current
branch and rewrites the index and the

85
00:06:32,605 --> 00:06:33,196
working tree.

86
00:06:33,536 --> 00:06:35,993
A merge commit, ec7d2b5,
although none was needed.

87
00:06:36,333 --> 00:06:41,196
The two branch commits form a visible bubble.
As a picture: main was on 6ae3c51, and

88
00:06:41,236 --> 00:06:43,590
the merge commit ec7d2b5 closes the loop.

89
00:06:43,930 --> 00:06:47,540
--no-commit. Same starting state,
a fast-forward is possible.

90
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:50,711
You want to look at the
result before it is recorded.

91
00:06:50,751 --> 00:06:54,121
Predict what git merge --no-commit
does. Say it out loud.

92
00:06:58,961 --> 00:07:02,994
It fast-forwarded. If that surprised
you, you're in good company.

93
00:07:03,034 --> 00:07:06,565
The branch moved, and there
was nothing to inspect first.

94
00:07:06,605 --> 00:07:11,765
The manual says so directly: there is no
way to stop those merges with --no-commit.

95
00:07:11,805 --> 00:07:14,271
Combined with --no-ff it stops reliably.

96
00:07:14,611 --> 00:07:18,486
"Stopped before committing as requested."
The state is that of a merge whose

97
00:07:18,526 --> 00:07:22,660
conflicts are all resolved: MERGE_HEAD
exists, HEAD has not moved, and MERGE_MODE

98
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:26,163
remembers no-ff. This is where you
run the tests on the merged tree.

99
00:07:26,503 --> 00:07:32,150
--ff-only. Now main has a commit of its
own, so the branches have diverged.

100
00:07:32,490 --> 00:07:35,909
"Not possible to fast-forward, aborting",
exit status 128, and the status is clean.

101
00:07:35,949 --> 00:07:39,285
Nothing moved. That's why --ff-only is the
safe way to bring a branch up to date.

102
00:07:39,625 --> 00:07:41,582
--squash. git merge --squash 🟡 CAUTION:
it changes the index and the working tree

103
00:07:41,622 --> 00:07:42,962
and makes no commit. Predict
which files appear in .git.

104
00:07:47,802 --> 00:07:52,385
AUTO_MERGE, ORIG_HEAD and
SQUASH_MSG. No MERGE_HEAD.

105
00:07:52,425 --> 00:07:59,586
So git merge --abort has nothing to abort,
and the way back is git reset --merge.

106
00:07:59,626 --> 00:08:06,254
SQUASH_MSG is a draft message. The
commit you make next is an ordinary one.

107
00:08:06,594 --> 00:08:13,559
4b0b1d4 has one parent, d80b525. The graph
shows the branch still hanging off the

108
00:08:13,599 --> 00:08:14,337
old fork.

109
00:08:14,677 --> 00:08:20,324
git branch --no-merged lists the branch,
and git branch -d refuses to delete it

110
00:08:20,364 --> 00:08:25,867
here, where the branch has no upstream.
The content of the branch is in main.

111
00:08:25,907 --> 00:08:27,307
Its commits are not.

112
00:08:27,647 --> 00:08:31,857
The trap. Asha adds a commit to the
same branch and you squash again.

113
00:08:31,897 --> 00:08:34,505
Before the command,
predict the merge base.

114
00:08:39,345 --> 00:08:42,478
The merge base is still
6ae3c51, the original fork.

115
00:08:42,518 --> 00:08:47,641
That's the missing parent link. So the
second merge compares both tips with the old

116
00:08:47,681 --> 00:08:52,555
base again. main "changed" the line,
through the squash, and so did the branch.

117
00:08:52,595 --> 00:08:53,675
That's a conflict.

118
00:08:54,015 --> 00:08:58,204
The replay stops here. What happens
after this point is your exercise.

119
00:08:58,544 --> 00:09:00,853
The configuration form, for completeness:

120
00:09:01,193 --> 00:09:03,587
The merge commit as an object.
labs/run ch08/merge-commit-anatomy.

121
00:09:03,927 --> 00:09:08,538
One tree, two parent lines,
author, committer, message.

122
00:09:08,578 --> 00:09:15,386
Nothing else. Read the two parents. The
first begins c089834, the commit on main.

123
00:09:15,426 --> 00:09:19,530
The second begins 58a5e60,
the tip of the branch.

124
00:09:19,870 --> 00:09:27,327
^1 and ^2 name them, the range ^1..^2 lists
the commits that came in, and ^- adds

125
00:09:27,367 --> 00:09:28,900
the merge itself.

126
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:35,178
--first-parent gives four lines, and the
two branch commits are not among them.

127
00:09:35,218 --> 00:09:39,491
--merges and --no-merges split
the history the other way.

128
00:09:39,831 --> 00:09:42,316
A merge commit has one diff per parent,
and they answer different questions.

129
00:09:42,656 --> 00:09:44,957
Against parent 1: what main
gained, the prompt lines.

130
00:09:44,997 --> 00:09:48,668
Against parent 2: what the feature branch
would have gained, the temperature change.

131
00:09:49,008 --> 00:09:53,398
The first-parent flip. Someone merges main
into feature/httpx and then fast-forwards

132
00:09:53,438 --> 00:09:56,773
main to it. Predict the first-parent
history of main afterwards.

133
00:10:04,613 --> 00:10:08,363
Before, the first-parent history of main
contained "Retry the judge three times".

134
00:10:08,703 --> 00:10:15,263
Afterwards that commit, cfc99c8, is on the
second-parent side, and the branch commit

135
00:10:15,303 --> 00:10:20,291
042925e, "Add httpx for the judge
client", sits on the mainline.

136
00:10:20,331 --> 00:10:26,341
Nothing is lost. But every tool that
reads the mainline now walks through the

137
00:10:26,381 --> 00:10:27,519
feature branch.

138
00:10:27,859 --> 00:10:28,219
Octopus. labs/run ch08/octopus.

139
00:10:28,169 --> 00:10:32,693
"Merge made by the 'octopus' strategy."

140
00:10:36,033 --> 00:10:40,665
Four parents: your tip first, then the
three branches in the order you named them.

141
00:10:41,005 --> 00:10:47,716
Now with a branch that touches
the same line as main.

142
00:10:47,756 --> 00:10:52,302
Predict the state after the failure.

143
00:10:57,142 --> 00:11:05,550
Exit status 2, no MERGE_HEAD, a clean status.
An octopus merge that hits a conflict

144
00:11:05,590 --> 00:11:14,100
is rolled back, not paused. Merge the
conflicting branch separately, resolve it, and

145
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:15,729
bundle the rest.

146
00:11:17,569 --> 00:11:19,217
Five mistakes to watch for.

147
00:11:19,557 --> 00:11:23,228
Squashing a branch, then continuing
to work on it and squashing again.

148
00:11:23,268 --> 00:11:27,575
Root cause: content was merged without
ancestry, so the merge base never moved and

149
00:11:27,615 --> 00:11:31,127
the second merge sees the delivered
lines as changed on both sides.

150
00:11:31,467 --> 00:11:37,455
Relying on --no-commit alone to inspect
a merge. Root cause: the option has no

151
00:11:37,495 --> 00:11:43,560
effect on a fast-forward, which needs no
commit; only --no-ff --no-commit stops

152
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:44,256
reliably.

153
00:11:44,596 --> 00:11:49,027
Running git merge --abort after
a --squash that you do not want.

154
00:11:49,067 --> 00:11:54,826
Root cause: a squash writes no MERGE_HEAD,
so there is no merge to abort; git reset

155
00:11:54,866 --> 00:11:56,502
--merge is the way back.

156
00:11:56,842 --> 00:12:00,553
Merging main into a feature branch
and fast-forwarding main to it.

157
00:12:00,593 --> 00:12:05,213
Root cause: first parent means "what was
checked out", not "main", so the mainline

158
00:12:05,253 --> 00:12:07,259
now runs through the feature branch.

159
00:12:07,599 --> 00:12:10,827
Starting an octopus merge
with a branch that conflicts.

160
00:12:10,867 --> 00:12:15,521
Root cause: the octopus strategy refuses
any conflict and rolls the whole merge

161
00:12:15,561 --> 00:12:15,818
back.

162
00:12:17,658 --> 00:12:20,204
Now, out of the lab. A backend team that
serves model inference merges every pull

163
00:12:20,244 --> 00:12:22,726
request into main. A pull request is
GitHub's proposal to merge one branch into

164
00:12:22,766 --> 00:12:25,312
another. When a deployment breaks at three
in the afternoon, the on-call engineer

165
00:12:25,352 --> 00:12:27,738
runs git log --first-parent --oneline
main. On this team that is the release

166
00:12:27,778 --> 00:12:29,718
history: one line per integrated
unit, in the order it landed.

167
00:12:29,758 --> 00:12:32,048
Each line is a candidate for git revert
-m 1, which a later video covers.

168
00:12:32,388 --> 00:12:36,132
That only works because of
two rules the team enforces.

169
00:12:36,172 --> 00:12:41,636
Integrate into main with --no-ff merges,
and never fast-forward main to a branch

170
00:12:41,676 --> 00:12:43,081
that has merged main.

171
00:12:43,421 --> 00:12:48,229
One step across the layer boundary. The
merge methods of a pull request map onto

172
00:12:48,269 --> 00:12:53,198
these options, but they run on GitHub's
servers and are not identical to the local

173
00:12:53,238 --> 00:12:58,166
commands. GitHub's documentation describes
the default method as a merge using the

174
00:12:58,206 --> 00:13:03,196
--no-ff option. For the squash method the
documentation carries the same warning as

175
00:13:03,236 --> 00:13:08,225
today's root-cause box: if you keep working
on the head branch, later pull requests

176
00:13:08,265 --> 00:13:12,346
can include commits that were already
squashed into the base branch.

177
00:13:12,386 --> 00:13:14,527
Chapter 17 covers the merge methods.

178
00:13:14,867 --> 00:13:20,740
The habits the textbook recommends. --ff-only
to update your local main, so that a

179
00:13:20,780 --> 00:13:26,149
surprise divergence stops you. --no-ff
to integrate a feature branch as one

180
00:13:26,189 --> 00:13:32,135
revertable unit. --squash when the branch
history is noise, then delete the branch.

181
00:13:32,175 --> 00:13:37,543
And --no-ff --no-commit to test the
merged tree before it becomes a commit.

182
00:13:39,383 --> 00:13:42,748
Your turn. Do Exercise 6.5, Level 2,
"what a squash leaves behind", in

183
00:13:42,788 --> 00:13:44,353
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

184
00:13:44,693 --> 00:13:45,890
Predict before you type:

185
00:13:46,230 --> 00:13:49,294
After a squash and a commit, how many
parents does the new commit have, and what

186
00:13:49,334 --> 00:13:51,389
does git merge-base between
main and the branch print?

187
00:13:51,729 --> 00:13:55,297
Which files in .git exist between
the squash and the commit?

188
00:13:55,637 --> 00:14:00,405
What will git branch --merged
and git branch --no-merged say?

189
00:14:00,745 --> 00:14:05,663
The challenge is Exercise 6.4, Level
2, "two merges", in the same file.

190
00:14:07,503 --> 00:14:14,786
Q131: "Compare git merge -X ours, git merge
-s ours and git merge --squash in terms

191
00:14:14,826 --> 00:14:16,991
of content and ancestry."

192
00:14:19,331 --> 00:14:27,193
Answer out loud before you open the answers
file. A strong answer uses exactly the

193
00:14:27,233 --> 00:14:35,287
two axes the question names and fills a
small grid: for each of the three, what ends

194
00:14:35,327 --> 00:14:41,358
up in the tree, and what parent
links the resulting commit has.

195
00:14:41,398 --> 00:14:49,452
It separates a strategy option from a
strategy. It names the pair that are opposites

196
00:14:49,492 --> 00:14:57,354
of each other and says why. And it finishes
with one practical consequence of each

197
00:14:57,394 --> 00:15:00,919
for a later merge of the same branch.

198
00:15:02,759 --> 00:15:05,687
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

199
00:15:06,027 --> 00:15:12,156
The merge options choose history, not content:
--ff-only moves nothing unless it can

200
00:15:12,196 --> 00:15:18,326
fast-forward, --no-ff always records a merge
commit, and --no-commit stops only when

201
00:15:18,366 --> 00:15:24,128
combined with --no-ff. A squash stages
the merged content and records no second

202
00:15:24,168 --> 00:15:29,931
parent, so the merge base does not move
and the branch cannot be reused safely.

203
00:15:30,271 --> 00:15:35,671
A merge commit is an ordinary commit with
more than one parent, and parent 1 is what

204
00:15:35,711 --> 00:15:41,046
was checked out. --first-parent reads the
mainline, and that mainline flips if main

205
00:15:41,086 --> 00:15:46,356
is fast-forwarded to a branch that merged
main. An octopus merge has three or more

206
00:15:46,396 --> 00:15:49,141
parents and is rolled
back on any conflict.

207
00:15:50,981 --> 00:15:54,722
Read sections 8.12 to 8.14 of Chapter 8.

208
